\section{Diagnostic Framework}\label{sec:method}

Our analysis is observational: we characterize statistical associations between agreement signals and output quality without claiming causal mechanisms.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}\label{sec:formulation}

Given model $\mathcal{M}$ and input $x$, we sample $N$ outputs $\{y_1, \ldots, y_N\}$ at temperature $T > 0$ via constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}, where each $y_i$ is an entity set.
A \textbf{per-sample signal} $c_{\text{ps}}: (x, y_i) \to \mathbb{R}$ scores each output independently; an \textbf{instance-level signal} $c_{\text{il}}: (x, \{y_i\}_{i=1}^N) \to \mathbb{R}$ produces a single score per input (notation in Appendix~\ref{app:notation}).
This distinction is consequential: 4 of 5 signals are instance-level aggregates assigning identical scores to all $N$ candidates.
Only LP carries per-sample discriminative power: ensemble optimization assigns LP mean weight $0.78$, with structural signals receiving zero weight (Appendix~\ref{app:supervised_combination}).
When LP fails---as in few-shot regimes where LP $\rho{\to}0$ (\S\ref{sec:scaling})---no alternative per-sample signal exists.

We define the \textbf{capability ceiling} as the practical performance upper bound achievable through self-consistency at realistic sample budgets ($N{\leq}16$), beyond which diminishing returns render further scaling impractical.

\subsection{Confidence Signals}\label{sec:signals}

Entity matching uses exact span and type match.
We distinguish two signal families: four \textit{agreement signals} computed across samples---\textit{structural} (Soft Jaccard [SJ], entity voting confidence [VC]) and \textit{surface} (exact match [EM], Fleiss' $\kappa$ [FK])---and log-probability [LP] as the sole \textit{model-internal} signal, derived from generation probabilities and providing per-sample discrimination.

\paragraph{Log-probability (per-sample).}
$c_{\text{lp}}(y_i) = |y_i|^{-1} \sum_{j} \log p_{\mathcal{M}}(t_j \mid t_{<j}, x)$.
Mean LP is preferred over cumulative sequence-level LP as length normalization eliminates length bias ($+4.6$ F1 points; Appendix~\ref{app:token_signal}).

\paragraph{Soft Jaccard (structural, instance-level).}
For samples $y_i, y_j$, we group by type, compute max-weight bipartite matching (Hungarian algorithm, token-level IoU):
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj}
\text{SJ}(y_i, y_j) = \frac{\sum_{m \in M} \text{sim}(a_m, b_m)}{\sum_{g} \max(|g_i|, |g_j|)}
\end{equation}
Instance-level QE averages over all pairs: $c_{\text{sj}}(x) = \binom{N}{2}^{-1} \sum_{i<j} \text{SJ}(y_i, y_j)$.
For selection, consensus-proximity converts to per-sample rankings via MBR (\S\ref{sec:mbr_theory}).

\paragraph{Other signals.}
EM: proportion of samples producing the most common output ($c_{\text{em}}(x) = N^{-1} \max_{y} |\{i : y_i = y\}|$).
FK: inter-rater agreement \cite{fleiss1971agreement} adapted to entity sets, treating unique (text, type) tuples as items with binary present/absent labels (Appendix~\ref{app:fk_independence}).
VC: mean entity frequency $\text{freq}(e) = |\{i : e \in y_i\}| / N$ across $E_\cup$.\footnote{When $E_\cup = \emptyset$, SJ${=}1$ (perfect agreement on empty output); $\text{VC}{=}0$.}

\subsection{Evaluation}\label{sec:evaluation}

\paragraph{Quality estimation.}
Spearman $\rho$ between signal and instance-level F1 (exact span-and-type match), and AUROC classifying above/below median F1.
Both exclude instances with no gold annotations: $n{=}529$ on SciERC, $n{=}2{,}756$ on CoNLL (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}).

\paragraph{Sample selection.}
Best-of-$N$ F1: LP ranks candidates directly; instance-level signals rank via consensus-proximity.
Baselines: \textit{oracle} (highest-F1 sample) and \textit{greedy} ($T{=}0$).
\textbf{Instance degeneracy}: fraction of instances where all $N$ samples yield identical F1;\footnote{``Identical F1'' means constant F1 across all $N$ samples for a given instance, not necessarily F1${=}1.0$. This includes three distinct cases: (1)~identical entity sets (true output collapse), (2)~different entity sets that happen to achieve the same F1 (coincidental F1 tie), and (3)~different entity boundaries with identical span-level scores. Entity-set exact-match degeneracy (case 1 only) is lower ($9$--$26$\% vs.\ $13$--$31$\% constant-F1; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}); we use constant-F1 as the operationally relevant definition since selection cannot improve F1 regardless of which case applies.} for these, no signal can improve selection.
We adopt \textit{degeneracy} (from statistical mechanics: multiple microstates mapping to identical macrostates) to distinguish from training convergence and to emphasize the many-to-one collapse of distinct samples onto identical entity sets.

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Test-time scaling and self-consistency.}\label{sec:yun_contrast}
Self-consistency \cite{wang2023selfconsistency} and best-of-$N$ with scaling laws \cite{snell2025scaling, brown2024monkeys, gao2023scaling} yield large gains on reasoning; recent extensions include reward-tail-guided search \cite{li2026rewardtail}. Reward-free selectors \cite{taubenfeld2025cisc, kang2025selfcertainty} and trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers, lightman2024verify, li2023stepaware} target discrete-answer settings.
SC can fail when consistent outputs are wrong \cite{chen2023twofailures}; free-form \cite{chen2024usc} and atomic \cite{thirukovalluru2024atomic} variants face limits where sub-output agreement does not yield selection gain. Iterative self-verification \cite{shinn2023reflexion} offers an alternative, but our ESJ and MRSC results (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}) show such approaches also fail for NER.
\citet{yun2025price} identify diversity collapse under constrained decoding; we extend their qualitative account with quantitative diagnostics (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).

\paragraph{Consistency for structured IE.}
\citet{yao2026consistency} show consistency correlates with accuracy for LLM-based NER/RE ($\rho$ up to 0.47); we extend to instance-level analysis: (1)~task-level $\rho$ masks instance-level degeneracy; (2)~we decompose failure into degeneracy and LP compression; (3)~pre-deployment diagnostics that task-level correlation cannot provide.
Critically, their task-level granularity cannot distinguish instances where scaling helps from those where it harms---our entity-level decomposition addresses this gap (Table~\ref{tab:novelty_comparison}).
\citet{xie2023zeroshot} propose entity voting for zero-shot NER; \citet{xie2024selfimproving} report positive SC where zero-shot diversity is high.

\paragraph{Uncertainty estimation and MBR.}
Our selection instantiates MBR decoding \cite{eikema2022sampling}; \citet{eikema2025structured} find MBR utilities degrade on structured outputs, consistent with our LP compression findings.
LP is well-calibrated at population level \cite{kadavath2022language, tian2023calibration} and motivated as a quality signal by noisy channel models \cite{yee2019noisy}, yet too compressed within instances for selection.
Complementary approaches include uncertainty estimation for autoregressive structured prediction \cite{malinin2021uncertainty}, calibration of pre-trained transformers \cite{desai2020calibration}, large-scale uncertainty quantification \cite{xiao2022uncertainty}, semantic uncertainty \cite{kuhn2023semantic}, selective prediction \cite{geifman2017selective}, and conformal prediction \cite{angelopoulos2023conformal}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Novelty comparison with closest related work along six dimensions.}
\label{tab:novelty_comparison}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Dimension & \citet{yao2026consistency} & \citet{yun2025price} & \citet{landesberg2026bon} & Ours \\
\midrule
Domain & NER/RE & Reasoning & Reasoning & NER/RE/SQL \\
Granularity & Task & Task & Prompt & Instance \\
Failure mechanism & --- & Diversity collapse & Baseline effects & Degeneracy $+$ $\alpha$ \\
Diagnostic framework & $\rho$ only & Qualitative & $r$ global/local & $D$, $G_{\text{nd}}$, $\alpha$, LP \\
Scale range & Single & --- & Single & 4B--72B+API \\
Prescriptive & No & No & No & Conditional ($p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.055$) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Concurrent work.}
\citet{landesberg2026bon} independently document a correlation-selection gap in best-of-$N$ reasoning: global judge $r{=}0.47$ masks within-prompt $r{=}0.27$ (Table~\ref{tab:novelty_comparison}).
This concurrent discovery validates the gap as cross-domain.
The studies diverge in four respects (Table~\ref{tab:novelty_comparison}):
\textit{(a)~domain}---reasoning (scalar judgments) vs.\ NER/RE (entity sets with partial overlap);
\textit{(b)~failure mechanism}---prompt-level baseline effects vs.\ degeneracy ($12$--$68$\%) and signal alignment collapse ($\alpha{\to}0$), mechanisms specific to structured prediction;
\textit{(c)~granularity}---prompt-level $r$ decomposition vs.\ instance- and entity-level diagnostics ($D$, $G_{\textup{nd}}$, $\alpha$);
\textit{(d)~prescriptive}---no constructive remedy vs.\ entity-level construction with MBR grounding (Proposition~\ref{prop:construction_mbr}).

\section{Single-Signal Selection Results}\label{sec:selection_gap}

\paragraph{Definitional vs.\ empirical.}
We decompose the correlation-selection gap into two layers.
The first is structural: four of five signals are instance-level aggregates that assign identical scores to all $N$ candidates from a given input (\S\ref{sec:formulation}), so they estimate quality across inputs but cannot rank candidates within an instance---selection failure is a design property, not an empirical finding.
The second layer is empirical and constitutes the core diagnostic: LP---the only per-sample signal---suffers compression under constrained decoding, with 60\% of SciERC instances showing within-instance LP range below 0.041 nats (Appendix~\ref{app:logprob_ties}), leaving insufficient variation for sample discrimination. This compression is model-intrinsic: free-form decoding yields nearly identical LP distributions (within-instance std ratio $0.97{\times}$; Appendix~\ref{app:freeform_ablation}). Even where LP varies, selection gains depend on LP-quality alignment rather than degeneracy alone.
Cross-instance $\rho$ (Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation}) is attenuated by varying instance difficulty; within-instance $\rho$ directly measures whether LP discriminates candidates for a fixed input, making it the relevant predictor of selection success.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ selection (macro F1) on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Selection signals: 2-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$; baselines: 3-seed (Oracle${=}0.781$, Greedy${=}0.667$). LP is per-sample; others use consensus-proximity. $^\dagger$One score per input.}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & $p$ \\
\midrule
Oracle & $0.775 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
Greedy ($T{=}0$) & $0.645 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
\midrule
LP           & $\mathbf{0.647} \pm 0.005$ & .29 \\
SJ           & $0.646 \pm 0.002$ & .67 \\
FK           & $0.646 \pm 0.001$ & .79 \\
EM           & $0.637 \pm 0.001$ & n/a$^\dagger$ \\
VC           & $0.623 \pm 0.004$ & n/a$^\dagger$ \\
\midrule
Random       & $0.610 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_selection_gap}
\caption{Correlation-selection gap on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Bars: QE $\rho$; line: $\Delta$F1 vs.\ greedy (3-seed mean). Despite $\rho$ up to 0.38, no signal outperforms greedy (all $p{\geq}0.29$). Dashed green: oracle headroom ($+11.4$\,pp; oracle headroom $=$ best-of-$N$ F1 $-$ greedy F1); dotted gray: random.}
\label{fig:selection_gap}
\end{figure}

Despite $\rho$ up to 0.38, no signal outperforms greedy on SciERC (Table~\ref{tab:selection}; Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}); the oracle shows 11.4\,pp headroom, but LP achieves $+0.2$\,pp ($p{=}0.29$), below MDE (${\approx}$1.5\,pp at $n{=}529$, 2--3 seeds; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).
All selection experiments use 2--3 training seeds; bootstrap CIs are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}.
The pattern holds across CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).
Few-NERD provides the clearest positive case: LP achieves $+1.39$\,pp ($p{<}0.001$; Holm $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.021$, selection family $k{=}21$)\footnote{Under global correction ($k{=}57$), LP-best selection reaches Holm $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.055$ (BH $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.003$).} at low degeneracy (13.0\% vs.\ 12.2--67.9\%), while structural signals still degrade (VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$\,pp; \S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).

\paragraph{Diagnostic framework.}
Three co-occurring factors jointly characterize the gap:
(1)~instance degeneracy sets the ceiling (SciERC 12.2\%, CoNLL 61.7\%);
(2)~oracle headroom bounds the opportunity within non-degenerate instances (CoNLL ${\approx}4$\,pp vs.\ SciERC 11.4\,pp);
(3)~LP compression limits exploitation (Few-NERD within-instance $\rho{\approx}0.5$--$0.7$ vs.\ SciERC $\rho{\approx}0.26$).
Degeneracy does not predict LP gain across datasets ($\rho{=}0.052$, $n{=}23$, $p{=}0.81$; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), though it co-varies with LP within fixed settings ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).
These conditions interact ($R^2{<}0$); on non-degenerate instances, $\alpha$ approaches zero ($0.000$--$0.161$) despite $\rho$ up to 0.45 (\S\ref{sec:theory}).

\paragraph{Mechanistic evidence (Appendix~\ref{sec:mechanistic}).}
Why does signal alignment collapse? Probing experiments on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct reveal an \textit{introspection boundary}: hidden-state linear probes achieve entity-level AUROC $0.63$--$0.88$, substantially exceeding prompt-based self-judgment (ESJ AUROC $0.51$; confirmed on Qwen3-8B: AUROC $0.50$--$0.55$; Appendix~\ref{app:esj}), yet cross-dataset transfer drops to $0.56$--$0.67$ (LODO).
Entity self-judgment, multi-round refinement ($-3.1$\,pp vs.\ greedy), and latent centroid selection ($-3.5$\,pp) all fail to improve over greedy decoding.
Entity quality is partially encoded in representations but inaccessible through the generation interface---consistent with the signal alignment collapse observed across all configurations.
Cross-model validation on Qwen3-8B confirms chance-level ESJ performance (AUROC $0.50$--$0.55$; Appendix~\ref{app:esj}), suggesting the introspection boundary is architecture-general rather than model-specific.

